(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-07"] = {"3.1":{"s":"Permutations and combinations are ways of counting arrangements and selections without listing them one by one. An arrangement in a fixed order is a permutation, and a selection where order does not matter is a combination. This chapter builds the counting rules, factorial notation, and the formulas for permutations and combinations."},"3.2":{"s":"Two rules do all the basic counting. The addition principle: if a job can be done in m ways or in n other ways, and these cannot happen together, there are m + n ways in all. The multiplication principle: if one step can be done in m ways and a second independent step in n ways, the two steps together can be done in m × n ways. Use 'or' for addition and 'and' for multiplication.","f":[{"x":"m + n","n":"Addition principle: either of two mutually exclusive events, with m and n ways.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.2, p.44"},{"x":"m*n","n":"Multiplication principle: two independent events together, with m and n ways.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.2, p.45"}],"w":["Add when the choices exclude each other (either one job or the other). Multiply when the steps follow each other.","Check that the events are really independent before multiplying."]},"3.3":{"s":"The invariance principle says that the number of ways does not change when you count the same things in a different order or by a different method. Choosing a pencil first and an eraser second gives the same total as choosing the eraser first. So when you use the addition or the multiplication principle, you may take the steps in any order and the answer stays the same."},"3.4":{"s":"The factorial of a positive whole number n is the product of all whole numbers from 1 up to n, written n!. It counts the ways to arrange n different objects in a row. By definition 0! is 1. A factorial grows very quickly. Larger factorials are written in terms of smaller ones: n! = n × (n − 1)!. This is used to cancel terms when simplifying fractions of factorials.","f":[{"x":"n! = n*(n - 1)*(n - 2)* ... *3*2*1","n":"Factorial of a positive whole number n.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.4, p.48"},{"x":"0! = 1","n":"Defined value; it keeps the formulas for permutations and combinations true.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.4, p.48"}],"w":["0! = 1, not 0. Also n! = n·(n − 1)!, so cancel the common factorial, never expand big ones.","Factorials are defined only for whole numbers (n ≥ 0)."]},"3.5":{"s":"A permutation is an arrangement of objects in a definite order. The number of arrangements of r objects chosen from n different objects is nPr. When some objects are alike, the total arrangements are divided by the factorials of the repeats. Arrangements in a circle have no fixed start, so a circle of n different objects has fewer arrangements than a row.","f":[{"x":"nPr = n!/(n - r)!","n":"Arrangements of r different objects out of n, no repetition, r <= n.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.5, p.51"},{"x":"n!/(n_1!*n_2!* ... *n_k!)","n":"Arrangements of n objects when n_1 are of one kind, n_2 of a second kind, and so on.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.5, p.56"},{"x":"(n - 1)!","n":"Circular arrangements of n different objects. If m of them are alike: (n - 1)!/m!.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.5, p.58"}],"w":["In a circle fix one object and arrange the rest: (n − 1)!, not n!.","With repeated objects, divide by the factorial of each repeat count."]},"3.6":{"s":"A combination is a selection of objects where order does not matter. The number of ways to choose r objects out of n different objects is nCr. It equals the number of permutations nPr divided by r!, because each selection can be arranged in r! orders. Choosing r objects to take is the same as choosing n − r to leave, so nCr = nC(n − r). Pascal's rule joins two neighbouring values.","f":[{"x":"nCr = n!/(r!*(n - r)!) = nPr/r!","n":"Selections of r different objects out of n, 0 <= r <= n.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.6, p.66"},{"x":"nCr = nC(n - r)","n":"Choosing r to take is the same as choosing n - r to leave. If nCx = nCy then x = y or x + y = n.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.6, p.67"},{"x":"nCr + nC(r - 1) = (n + 1)Cr","n":"Pascal's rule, 1 <= r <= n.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.6, p.67"},{"x":"2^n - 1","n":"Number of ways to select one or more things out of n different things.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, 3.6, p.67"}],"w":["Use permutation when order matters (arrange, rank, form a number); use combination when it does not (select, choose a team).","nCx = nCy gives two cases: x = y or x + y = n."]}};
